Description:
 ros-jazzy-legged-estimation - State estimation of legged robot
Changes:
 ros-jazzy-legged-estimation (3.5.0-2026.09.02.14.16) noble; urgency=high
 .
   * Harden the estimator against singular Jacobians and NaN
     - Damp the contact wrench pseudo-inverse and solve it as symmetric
     normal equations instead of forming an explicit inverse. The stacked
     Jacobian loses a rank when a knee reaches full extension; measured on
     a Jacobian with this robot's structure, the undamped solve reports
     -2.7e5 N on a foot carrying 320 N at a knee angle of 1e-6 rad, and
     chatters by an order of magnitude frame to frame. Damping by
     1e-8 * max(diag) holds it within 176-520 N across the whole sweep and
     is indistinguishable from the reference at the nominal posture. The
     LDLT solve is also 2.6x faster than the inverse and keeps the residual
     least-squares optimal; it does not by itself fix the amplification.
     - Zero the contact probabilities that are not finite, at the point they
     are produced. The existing gain guard could not prevent NaN reaching
     p_: both the process noise and the measurement noise are divided by
     the probability before any guard runs, so the gain came back entirely
     NaN and poisoned xHat_ and p_ with nothing to reset them. Negating the
     gain test as well keeps a NaN out of the "in contact" branch.
     - Veto contact explicitly for a foot with no normal force, instead of
     relying on a comparison against a divided-by-zero ZMP, and compare
     with std::abs. The unqualified abs bound to the double overload only
     by way of a transitive using-declaration.
     - Note that feetHeights_ is never written, so the contact height
     measurement is a constant zero, i.e. flat ground.
     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
